# Recipe Book visual alignment TODO:
    Source proposal: [Recipe Book visual alignment proposal](RecipeBookVisualAlignmentProposal.md).
    Compatibility contract: [Recipe Book design](RecipeBook.md).

    ## Goal:
        Align the Recipe Book with Design A using shared, opt-in styled native controls, attached folder tabs, compact composition, and the approved recipe-grid adaptations.
        Preserve the updated Tools icon, native interactions, inventory ownership, placement authority, and existing persistence formats.
        This is an unexecuted implementation plan. Creating it does not approve the proposal's unresolved adaptations or authorize implementation.

    ## Execution contract:
        Phase source references govern their tasks and subtasks. The complete proposal governs visual targets and exclusions; the design guide governs unchanged feature semantics.
        Use the repository's existing test fixtures and build tooling. Keep game-source references read-only and new mod-internal controls internal.
        Use native per-instance settings where sufficient and narrow rendering adapters elsewhere. Do not introduce global GUI mutations, production reflection, a general theme framework, or changes to unrelated consumers.
        Every checkbox begins unchecked. Existing code and earlier passing tests are starting evidence, not completion of this plan.
        Run focused validation for each changed surface; broaden only when dependency changes or failures require it. Unit fixtures are not live GPU or in-game evidence.
        A blocked approval or native limitation leaves affected work unchecked. Do not silently substitute a different appearance or claim full alignment with outstanding requirements.

    ## Assumptions and open questions:
        The proposal remains a draft. Shared reusable controls and retention of the current Tools artwork are established directions, not open alternatives.
        Approval is still required for responsive square tiles with minimum five-unit gaps, horizontal-only list padding, quieter availability outlines, the separate gold selection marker, inset previews, and local dialog/titlebar adapters.
        The exact selection marker and preview inset are not specified. Determine the marker before its implementation; obtain approval of the preview inset through visual review before acceptance.
        Applicable non-tab hover, pressed, disabled, focused, checked, and opened treatments need concrete definitions consistent with the proposal palette.
        Native focus highlighting is composer-controlled and non-virtual. A local, non-duplicating focus treatment is a feasibility gate, not an assumed subclass override.
        Native popup row assumptions and non-virtual recomposition must be reconciled before a compact popup is accepted.
        The visual reference is Design A in the existing recipe-tab-mockups.html session artifact. Preserve access to that reference for comparison; the proposal overrides its outdated Tools glyph and distinguishes placeholder content.
        Do not add recipe features, change filtering or sort defaults, alter category membership, add movement modes, change transfer rules, replace icon artwork, or persist new state.

    ## Phase 1: Confirm the implementation and acceptance contract:
        Prerequisites: None.
        Sources: Proposal sections "Purpose and design authority", "Scope and invariants", "Decisions requiring approval", and "Acceptance and verification"; the design guide's lifecycle and authority contracts.
        Outcome: Implementation can proceed against explicit decisions without treating mockup placeholders or unresolved recommendations as approved behavior.

        ☐ Confirm outstanding approvals and record their disposition in the authoritative proposal.
            ☐ Confirm minimum-46-unit responsive square tiles, minimum-five-unit gaps, and the five-column baseline target.
            ☐ Confirm six-unit horizontal list padding without a reserved vertical padding strip.
            ☐ Confirm one-unit availability outlines and choose a distinct gold selection marker that cannot obscure availability or quantities.
            ☐ Confirm the inset-preview direction and arrange visual approval of its exact inset.
            ☐ Confirm the permitted local dialog/titlebar adaptations; leave native feasibility questions explicitly unresolved until Phase 2.
            ☐ Define applicable non-tab state treatments and objective visual expectations without inventing new interaction semantics.
        ☐ Establish the evidence baseline.
            ☐ Preserve access to Design A and the current category assets, including the updated Tools SVG.
            ☐ Identify the target game/API version and distinguish reference-source evidence from the client/package used for live acceptance.
            ☐ Record applicable existing test results and identify gaps for the changed controls, geometry, rendering, and lifecycle.
            ☐ Keep permanent decisions in the proposal and required acceptance evidence in its agreed destination; do not create a separate planning diary.

        Completion criteria:
            ☐ Required design choices have explicit approval or an explicitly blocked disposition; blocked choices do not permit dependent implementation.
            ☐ The visual reference, target environment, acceptance states, and preserved contracts are identifiable to the executing agent.

    ## Phase 2: Establish shared visual foundations and resolve native risks:
        Prerequisites: Phase 1's approvals for the affected work.
        Sources: Proposal sections "Visual specification", "Shared control architecture", "Native rendering constraints", and "Outer chrome and integration".
        Outcome: Tabs and core-control adapters can consume verified shared drawing, measurement, and focus mechanisms.

        ☐ Define shared named palette values, dimensions, and narrowly scoped drawing helpers in the shared GUI layer.
            ☐ Cover every palette role in the proposal, with GUI-scaled outline/accent thickness and whole-physical-pixel rasterization.
            ☐ Keep reusable control styling independent of Recipe Book session types, localization keys, catalogue state, and persistence.
            ☐ Separate generic control measurements from dialog-specific composition, while exposing measurements needed by minimum sizing, popup placement, and tooltip bounds.
            ☐ Share rounding and baseline rules without introducing a configurable theme framework.
        ☐ Resolve risky native rendering seams through focused fixtures or minimal local probes before broad migration.
            ☐ Verify borderless native button rendering can retain activation, text, visibility, enabled state, and native callbacks with custom chrome.
            ☐ Verify editable bounds and placeholder presentation can satisfy the eight-unit inset without inaccessible fields or misaligned text/caret/selection.
            ☐ Verify dropdown/popup adapters retain their appearance after opening, selection, and list replacement despite non-virtual recomposition.
            ☐ Reconcile any changed popup row geometry with native hit testing and scrolling; do not independently shrink rendered rows.
            ☐ Demonstrate a local focus integration with no competing native outline, including the category strip's one outer tab stop.
            ☐ Verify native dialog/titlebar composition can support the specified chrome while retaining movement and close behavior.
            ☐ Surface any restriction to the user for approval; do not add reflection, global patches, or silent visual fallbacks.
        ☐ Add independent style and geometry expectations in GUI tests rather than importing production constants as expected results.

        Completion criteria:
            ☐ Shared helpers reproduce approved dimensions and palette roles with correct physical-pixel behavior.
            ☐ Native-risk probes provide evidence for each proposed adapter boundary; unresolved restrictions block their consumers.
            ☐ Focus integration and dialog/titlebar adaptation have an approved, locally scoped implementation path.

    ## Phase 3: Align category tabs and presentation:
        Prerequisites: Phases 1 and 2. Independent of Phase 4.
        Sources: Proposal sections "Palette and tab states" and "Category presentation".
        Outcome: The tab strip matches the approved state treatment and exposes complete, stable category presentation to dialog composition.

        ☐ Update GuiElementIconTab rendering without replacing correct category artwork.
            ☐ Use the approved idle, hover, and selected icon tints with identical 20-by-20 GUI-unit display geometry.
            ☐ Add the inactive hover fill, scaled outlines, and separate two-unit selected upper accent.
            ☐ Keep selected tabs 36 units high with an open bottom edge, inactive tabs 32 units high with lower shoulders, and inactive bottom borders matching the panel.
            ☐ Preserve selected treatment on hover and add the approved thin inset dashed warm-white category-focus treatment through the verified local integration.
            ☐ Coordinate tab/panel baseline snapping during fractional dragging and recomposition; preserve nearest magnification and texture ownership.
        ☐ Update GuiElementIconTabs strip geometry and Recipe Book category ordering.
            ☐ Use 30-unit tab widths and four-unit inter-tab gaps without extra leading/trailing spacing.
            ☐ Order present standard categories All, Construction, Decorative, Mechanics, Terrain, Tools, Clothing, Items, Other.
            ☐ Insert additional categories before Other, after the standard named categories, sorted by localized name with an ordinal code tie-breaker.
            ☐ Preserve code-based selection/restoration, category membership, localized headings/tooltips, wrapping, and shared-host overflow navigation.
        ☐ Extend GUI and category lifecycle tests.
            ☐ Assert approved raster colors, accent, seam continuity, stationary icons, and fractional-scale geometry independently.
            ☐ Cover omitted categories, additional categories, translated names/ties, restored identity, keyboard/mouse selection, and resource disposal.

        Completion criteria:
            ☐ The strip width matches tab widths plus inter-tab gaps, and the first tab is flush with the panel edge.
            ☐ All required tab states meet the specified palette and geometry without moving icons or duplicating focus outlines.
            ☐ Ordering changes preserve membership, restoration, and generic strip interaction behavior.

    ## Phase 4: Implement reusable styled native controls:
        Prerequisites: Phases 1 and 2. Independent of Phase 3.
        Sources: Proposal section "Shared control architecture", including its native constraints and ownership requirements.
        Outcome: Recipe Book composition can adopt independently tested shared controls without feature dependencies.

        ☐ Implement the shared styled text-button adapter.
            ☐ Retain native borderless text and interaction rendering; supply flat backgrounds, outlines, and approved state overlays.
            ☐ Preserve native click consumption, keyboard activation, active/pressed behavior, enabled/visible state, and text measurement/padding.
        ☐ Generalize GuiElementRecipeBookSearch into a shared styled clearable text input.
            ☐ Accept caller-provided placeholder, clear-tooltip text, font/style inputs, and text-change callback.
            ☐ Preserve native editing, text selection, caret, focus, clear visibility, pointer cursor, and clear-button press/release behavior.
            ☐ Give the shared component ownership of clear textures and tooltip lifecycle without Recipe Book localization or controller dependencies.
            ☐ Keep the old feature-specific class only until Phase 5 migrates its consumers and tests; do not maintain two permanent implementations.
        ☐ Implement shared styled dropdown and popup-list adapters.
            ☐ Reuse the item-stack dropdown's owned-list replacement pattern without introducing item-preview behavior.
            ☐ Style both the closed selector and expanded list, including the compact chevron and approved states.
            ☐ Preserve native selection, callbacks, focus loss, opening/closing, keyboard/mouse navigation, wheel handling, and scrolling.
            ☐ Keep custom rendering authoritative across recomposition and list replacement, and dispose replaced/native/additional owned resources correctly.
        ☐ Implement the shared styled checkbox adapter.
            ☐ Draw the flat frame and checked state while retaining native toggling, focus, keyboard/mouse activation, and callbacks.
            ☐ Keep the 17-unit visual checkbox and reliable input bounds coordinated with measurement.
        ☐ Add focused reusable-control tests in the GUI unit-test area.
            ☐ Exercise applicable idle/hover/pressed/disabled/focused/checked/opened states and approved styling.
            ☐ Test clear-input behavior with arbitrary caller labels and callbacks without constructing Recipe Book state.
            ☐ Verify clearing produces the normal text-change notification exactly once and retains expected focus.
            ☐ Cover text/caret/selection geometry, long labels, list recomposition, popup input bounds, fractional scales, and texture/tooltip disposal.

        Completion criteria:
            ☐ All four control types are reusable, opt-in native-derived adapters with no Recipe Book semantic dependencies.
            ☐ Control state and rendering tests pass, including recomposition and clear-input callback guarantees.
            ☐ Existing unrelated consumers remain on their original controls with unchanged appearance and behavior.

    ## Phase 5: Adopt shared controls and align dialog composition:
        Prerequisites: Phases 3 and 4, plus Phase 2's approved local chrome path.
        Sources: Proposal sections "Dimensions and typography", "Controls and content composition", and "Outer chrome and integration"; the design guide's lifecycle and controls contracts.
        Outcome: The Recipe Book shell matches the mockup's compact structure while preserving the existing grid behavior for Phase 6.

        ☐ Update GuiDialogRecipeBook composition and RecipeBookLayout measurements together.
            ☐ Use preferred width 340 and approximately 420 height; grow minimum dimensions for complete tabs, translated headings/controls, empty-state content, and footer groups.
            ☐ Apply the 36-unit titlebar with 14-unit bold text, 12-unit gap before tabs, eight-unit outer border-box spacing, and specified content-panel padding.
            ☐ Keep the localized 11-unit category heading with at least 17-unit line space and eight-unit separation.
            ☐ Compose Hide uncraftable and the compact 12-unit sort selector on one row, remove the separate visible Sort by row, and retain localized explanation/labeling.
            ☐ Measure checkbox/label/selector separation without overlap; keep Hide uncraftable wording and Availability as the initial sort policy.
            ☐ Adopt the shared clearable input with Recipe Book-provided Search outputs placeholder, clear tooltip, and existing search callback.
            ☐ Apply 28-unit minimum search height, 14-unit text, eight-unit text inset, and ten-unit separation below.
            ☐ Align search, recessed list, and footer horizontal edges; remove the list frame outset and reserve scrollbar space internally.
            ☐ Retain one full-height inner viewport for scissor, layout, virtualization, input, and scrolling; do not introduce vertical-padding clipping.
        ☐ Compose the flat footer and recovery controls with shared buttons.
            ☐ Group Previous, Auto, and Next visually on the left while keeping distinct hit regions, enabled states, tooltips, and keyboard actions.
            ☐ Center the real alternative counter in the footer and place Handbook on the right; reserve symmetric space sufficient to avoid overlaps.
            ☐ Apply 12-unit regular footer text, eight-unit horizontal button padding, and 12-unit separation above.
            ☐ Style Clear search and Show uncraftable empty-state actions consistently without changing which controller they update.
        ☐ Integrate options and outer chrome locally.
            ☐ Use the shared checkbox in the options popup while retaining persisted preference handling, popup precedence, Escape/outside-click/focus-loss dismissal, and localized hints.
            ☐ Match approved dialog/titlebar gradients, outlines, separator, shadow, and text hierarchy without replacing native Fixed/Movable or close behavior.
            ☐ Retain and compatibly style scrollbar, options, movement, and close controls; derive popup and icon/tooltip geometry from shared measurements.
        ☐ Complete the clear-input migration after verifying its consumers.
            ☐ Update composition accessors, placeholder assignment, focus/clear paths, and directly affected tests to use the shared component.
            ☐ Remove GuiElementRecipeBookSearch and obsolete compatibility wrappers only after targeted validation passes.
            ☐ Keep maintained current-state documentation accurate for the migrated component without appending implementation history.
        ☐ Extend Recipe Book lifecycle and minimum-layout tests.
            ☐ Cover complete tabs at baseline width, long translations, additional categories, centered-footer overlap bounds, and constrained screens.
            ☐ Preserve search-first tab order, explicit-open search focus, automatic reopen without focus theft, and no focus acquisition during resize.
            ☐ Cover Fixed's two-physical-pixel docking gap, native Movable coordinates/dragging, reopening/recomposition, inventory closure, and popup precedence.
            ☐ Preserve category/search/sort and world-local browsing restoration, fresh-dialog craftable-only state, empty-state actions, hidden-selection disablement, and Handbook behavior.

        Completion criteria:
            ☐ Shell geometry, typography, controls, and palette match approved targets with no cropped labels or overlapping footer groups.
            ☐ Footer/recovery buttons and both checkbox locations use shared controls; the old search class has no remaining code/test consumers.
            ☐ Native lifecycle, controller callbacks, transfer authority, and persistence behavior remain unchanged.
            ☐ Existing square-grid behavior remains functional until Phase 6; responsive-grid fidelity is not claimed here.

    ## Phase 6: Implement approved responsive grid and recipe presentation:
        Prerequisites: Phase 5 and explicit Phase 1 approval of grid, padding, availability, and selection choices.
        Sources: Proposal section "Proposed recipe-grid adaptations"; the design guide's rendering, interaction, and browsing-state contracts.
        Outcome: Real recipes fill the aligned viewport with coherent responsive geometry and readable independent status, selection, focus, and quantities.

        ☐ Introduce one shared tile geometry consumed by GuiElementRecipeBookTiles, GuiElementRecipeBookTile, RecipeBookIconRenderer, quantities, and scrolling.
            ☐ Subtract approved horizontal padding and scrollbar space, select the largest fitting column count, and distribute spare width across square tiles.
            ☐ Enforce minimum 46-unit tiles and five-unit gaps, with five balanced columns at the approved baseline layout and no unused width strip.
            ☐ Assign whole-physical-pixel edges without cumulative drift; derive row pitch, visible rows, icon quads, and hit testing from the same geometry.
            ☐ Use six-unit horizontal padding, no reserved vertical strip, and clipping to the inner top/bottom edges, including partial rows.
            ☐ Preserve selected/inspected recipe identity on resize and restore or clamp scroll through recipe-row position rather than obsolete fixed pitches.
            ☐ Reconcile scrollbar/membership changes and world-local reopened scroll with responsive geometry without adding a persistence format.
        ☐ Update tile and shader presentation using the approved status/selection treatment.
            ☐ Use warm neutral available outlines, one-unit outer red/yellow availability outlines, and the existing unsupported disabled treatment and warning.
            ☐ Render the approved separate gold selection marker and independent keyboard focus without hiding availability or quantity text.
            ☐ Center real native-atlas previews using the proposed inset; obtain visual approval of the exact inset before marking presentation accepted.
            ☐ Keep output quantities visible and preserve icon preparation, atlas ownership, overlay depth, scissor unwinding, and reload/disposal behavior.
        ☐ Add geometry, rendering, and interaction regression tests.
            ☐ Test baseline/wider/constrained widths, column thresholds, independently expected tile edges/gaps, partial rows, and fractional scaling.
            ☐ Test resize/membership scroll reconciliation, scrollbar clamping, selected/inspected identity, and cancelled stale tile presses.
            ☐ Test available/missing/partial/unsupported/selected/focused combinations and output counts without conflating selection with availability.
            ☐ Verify mouse and keyboard placement still use live validation and existing controller actions, not displayed eligibility alone.

        Completion criteria:
            ☐ Baseline columns, minimum dimensions/gaps, horizontal distribution, and full-height clipping satisfy the approved measurable targets.
            ☐ All geometry consumers agree after resize and scrolling; real previews, quantities, status, selection, and focus remain legible.
            ☐ The exact preview inset has visual approval and rendering/interaction tests pass without changed allocation, filtering, or transfer semantics.

    ## Phase 7: Final review, acceptance, and document cleanup:
        Prerequisites: Phases 3 through 6 complete, with all applicable approval and native-feasibility gates resolved.
        Sources: Proposal sections "Acceptance and verification" and "Exclusions"; repository build/test conventions.
        Outcome: Full alignment is supported by source, test, package, and live-runtime evidence, with accurate permanent documentation and no temporary implementation debris.

        ☐ Review proposal coverage and run required regression validation.
            ☐ Reconcile every proposal requirement against implementation and evidence; distinguish remaining defects, evidence gaps, and explicitly approved scope changes.
            ☐ Run the focused GUI/Recipe Book suites covering changed surfaces and their dependent lifecycle, rendering, filtering, ordering, allocation, and transfer contracts.
            ☐ Reuse existing mocks and independently specified visual expectations; do not equate fixture success with GPU/runtime acceptance.
            ☐ Use the existing Cake/build.ps1 pipeline to validate and package the changed mod and shader/assets, recording the resulting package identity.
            ☐ Confirm the updated Tools and other category SVG artwork are unchanged, the old search component/temporary wrappers are removed, and unrelated consumers are unaffected.
        ☐ Collect comparable in-game visual and interaction evidence from the identified package.
            ☐ Capture idle, hover, selected, and keyboard-focused states at UI scales 1, 1.5, and 2; add fractional-scale geometry checks.
            ☐ Compare against Design A at the same category, normalized scale, and equivalent interaction states, using real previews and approved placeholder departures.
            ☐ Exercise closed/open dropdowns, checkbox states, clearable input, footer/recovery buttons, quantities, scrollbar, options, movement, and close controls.
            ☐ Exercise long translations, additional categories, constrained screen space, recomposition, partial-row scrolling, resizing, reopening, and inventory transitions.
            ☐ Verify native Fixed/Movable positioning, focus/input routing, Handbook, placement authorization, and relevant multiplayer/modpack compatibility separately from unit fixtures.
            ☐ Preserve required captures and acceptance decisions; unresolved visual differences or missing live evidence prevent a full-alignment claim.
        ☐ Update affected permanent documentation and completion tracking.
            ☐ Update RecipeBook.md's shared-control ownership, current geometry, rendering, and verification sections to describe the implemented design without a changelog.
            ☐ Repair references to the removed search class and add accurate links to the shared replacement; retain the authoritative proposal and this plan.
            ☐ Reassess the existing Recipe Book tab-redesign item in project.todo against complete visual acceptance; do not silently certify partial work.
        ☐ Inventory and remove temporary implementation-support documents and artifacts.
            ☐ Inventory working notes, interim analyses, handoff drafts, probe documentation, and other temporary documents created during Phases 1 through 6; none are designated for removal at plan creation.
            ☐ Preserve still-required decisions and evidence in the proposal, permanent guide, tests, or required acceptance-evidence destination before removing temporary documents.
            ☐ Update references, then remove only identified temporary implementation-support documents and verified obsolete probe/compatibility scaffolding.
            ☐ Remove task-created temporary validation outputs only after identifying their exact paths and preserving required evidence; do not delete unrelated or user-owned work.
            ☐ Retain Design A's authoritative reference artifact, the proposal, this plan, permanent project documentation, and required acceptance captures/results.

        Completion criteria:
            ☐ Every in-scope proposal requirement has implementation and appropriate evidence; approved scope changes are explicit and unapproved omissions remain blockers.
            ☐ Focused tests and package validation pass, and normalized live captures plus interaction checks support the approved appearance.
            ☐ Shared controls remain reusable and opt-in; no global styling changes, production reflection, new feature semantics, or icon replacements were introduced.
            ☐ Current-state documentation and completion tracking are accurate; temporary documents/scaffolding are inventoried, required content preserved, obsolete material removed, and references repaired.
            ☐ User visual acceptance is recorded before declaring full alignment.
